LAB — Solved ✅

Vulnerability

Unprotected administrative functionality / broken access control

Why it worked

The application relied on the administrative URL being difficult to discover rather than properly enforcing authorization.

Knowing or discovering the URL was enough to reach the administrative functionality.

The "robots.txt" file unintentionally helped reveal the hidden path, but the underlying security issue was the lack of proper access control on the administrator panel.

Key Lesson

Hiding a sensitive endpoint is not the same as protecting it.

Files such as "robots.txt" can reveal interesting paths, but even if an attacker discovers an administrative endpoint through another method, the application must still enforce authorization.

Sensitive functionality should verify that the requesting user has the required privileges before allowing administrative actions.

Bug Hunting Takeaway

When testing authorized applications, don't only look at what is visible in the interface.

Look for:

- Hidden or unlinked endpoints
- "robots.txt"
- Administrative paths
- Debug pages
- Forgotten functionality
- Direct URL access
- Missing authorization checks

The important question is:

«What happens if I directly request functionality that should only be available to an administrator?»

